\section{Attachment Is Not the Same as Permission to Execute}

A library can call a bridge from a worker that the VM did not
create. Native thread-local storage on that worker does not by
itself contain a runtime thread record, valid stack bounds or an
appropriate local-root frame. Runtime attachment is the protocol
for creating or associating that record. A runtime may also
require the thread to enter a managed execution region under a
lock, or may require all interpreter operations to run on an owner
thread regardless of attachment. Those are separate restrictions.
Attaching a worker does not make an owner-only object callable from
the worker.

Crestani's survey \cite{crestaniFFI} identifies cooperation between the FFI and the
collector on multithreaded heaps: another thread can collect while
external code runs, and uncoordinated descriptor access can become
invalid. The extension-language C API survey notes that
multithreading complicates access to JNI strings and arrays even
when primitive type mappings are straightforward. A bridge cannot
use a thread's apparent success with a scalar callback as evidence
that passing managed references the same way is valid.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.59\linewidth}}
\hline
Arrival state & Required transition \\
\hline
Worker not attached & Register if allowed, otherwise use a mailbox. \\
Attached, outside VM & Enter managed region with the runtime's protocol. \\
Managed thread in outbound call & Reenter under the saved foreign frame. \\
Attached to a different VM & Do not reuse the existing thread context. \\
VM shutting down & Reject or drain under a shutdown admission protocol. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Choose Direct Entry or a Mailbox Explicitly}

Consider a meter library that reports pairs of native integers from
a worker. If delivery is asynchronous and cannot return a decision,
the callback can copy the two integers to a native mailbox. The VM
owner drains the mailbox. Define coalescing, queue limits and what
to do on shutdown as part of the operation. For example, it is
reasonable to substitute newer samples for previously queued
samples in a live display, but incorrect to substitute them in a
recording operation promising every sample. Do not enqueue a raw
pointer to managed data or a native view that expires on callback
return.

When the C API demands a decision immediately, a mailbox with a
synchronous reply can deadlock. Suppose the VM owner has called
\texttt{meter\_flush} and waits for its worker. If the worker
cannot finish until the owner consumes a decision request, neither
thread can progress. Direct attachment only helps if the VM and
application permit the worker to run the closure, and if the owner
does not retain a lock it needs. \Cref{fig:visual-391} distinguishes
the two legitimate designs; a blocking relay is not automatically
equivalent to either.

