\section{Make Each Entry Scope Own Exactly Its Transitions}

\begin{verbatim}
decide_entry(ctx, sample):
    admission := ctx.vm.try_admit_callback()
    if admission is None: return STOP
    with admission:
        attachment := attach_this_thread_if_needed(ctx.vm)
        if attachment is failure: return STOP
        try:
            with enter_or_reenter_managed(attachment):
                with local_root_frame() as roots:
                    try:
                        handler := roots.load_global(ctx.handler)
                        arg := roots.box_sample(sample)
                        return CONTINUE if handler(arg) else STOP
                    catch recoverable_host_error:
                        ctx.record_first_error_without_throw()
                        return STOP
        finally:
            if attachment.was_created_here:
                detach_this_thread(attachment)
\end{verbatim}

Assume the native registrar keeps \texttt{ctx} live until callback
quiescence, and entry and exit helpers expose failures without
unwinding into C. The local root frame is closed before detachment.
A callback entered on a thread already registered may reuse the
attachment but has no right to detach that thread. Parent and
nested entries each get distinct local frames. A thread-local
context handle cannot simply be captured at registration on the
owner and replayed later on a foreign worker; store the VM
identity and resolve the context of the actual arriving thread.

For frequent invocations on a persistent worker, amortize
attachment only when a guaranteed thread-exit hook can detach and
the VM can coordinate termination with that worker. Attachment
and entry can have different costs: an idle attached worker may
leave managed execution so that it is not considered to be executing
managed instructions while waiting in a native library. A collector
may require a foreign-state transition to know which roots are
stable and whether the thread should participate in a safepoint.
Do not implement the transition by just setting a user-level flag.

\section{Prove Shutdown Against an Entry in Progress}

Define closing to prevent new admissions and let admitted callbacks
leave before destroying runtime state. A VM pointer must remain
valid long enough for a rejected late entry to observe closing, so
the supplier must also acknowledge that native delivery has
stopped. Use a two-phase stop: close admission, request native
stop, then wait for quiescence in a context that does not hold
the runtime lock needed by admitted workers. Only afterward can
the registrar release its roots and its reference to the VM.

Drive the test with barriers, not arbitrary sleep durations.
Pause a worker immediately before admission, trigger closing, then
release the barrier and observe a native stop result with no heap
access. Repeat with a barrier just after admission; destruction must
wait while that worker unwinds its entry stack. Finally drive a
callback on an already attached thread while that thread is in an
outbound call and verify that the VM's managed-to-foreign state is
restored, and that returning to the original host caller still uses
its own root frame.

\section{Keep the Reentry Stack and Runtime Lock in Agreement}

A same-thread nested callback creates an alternating stack:
host call, foreign entry, managed callback, another foreign entry,
and possibly another managed callback. Track the transition
state as nested scopes rather than a thread-wide Boolean. At the
deepest return, restore the foreign state of the inner call.
After that call returns to its managed caller, restore the
callback's managed state. Clearing a global in-callback flag at
the first return would leave the outer callback outside the
state the VM expects. The root-map discussion in
\Cref{ch:ext-411} explains the underlying constraint: the
collector needs locations that remain valid during each
transition, including suspended outer frames.

Consider a library that holds its own meter mutex while asking
the callback for a decision. The callback can attach correctly
and still deadlock if it calls an operation that tries to
acquire that mutex. This failure is not a collector bug.
Either the library drops its lock before invoking callbacks,
or the binding explicitly excludes those reentrant operations.
Test the restriction with a callback that invokes the disallowed
method and confirm it receives a host error without entering
the native operation. The same test on an unrelated meter can
establish whether the restriction is per object or per library.
